  "sourceNotes": {
    "raw": "The particle '喎' (wo3) at sentence end softens observations and invites agreement, commonly used when making gentle comments about someone's state. '好似' (hou2 chi5) means 'seems like' and creates tentative statements, showing politeness when discussing sensitive topics.\n\nThe phrase '方唔方便講' (fong1 m4 fong1 bin6 gong2) literally asks 'convenient not convenient to say' and demonstrates typical Cantonese A-not-A question pattern. This construction respects boundaries by acknowledging the listener may not want to share.\n\n'啱啱' (ngaam1 ngaam1) means 'just now/recently' and reduplicates the character for emphasis. When discussing sad news like '過咗身' (gwo3 jo2 san1 - passed away), Cantonese speakers use euphemistic language rather than direct terms, showing cultural sensitivity around death.\n\nPronunciation tip: '唔係' (m4 hai6) contracts naturally in speech, almost sounding like 'm-hai' as one syllable. Practice the high tone on '係' to distinguish negation from affirmation."
  }
